{"id": "lesson_20586d86a285", "claim": "At Mac session start after a Win session, always read REVIEW_QUEUE.md FIRST — the Win session completes tasks that Mac thought were pending. Never repeat work the Win session already did.", "conditions": ["Mac session", "REVIEW_QUEUE", "Win session", "cross-platform", "session handoff"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-06-28T07:13:51.672903+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-06-28T15:13:51.720521", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "2026-06-28: Win session had completed Hermes Phase 6+9, canary verification, and multiple memory entries before Mac session started. REVIEW_QUEUE.md was the ground truth. Mac session confirmed all tasks were done and proceeded directly to the next gate (cross-probe + T5 tagging) without re-doing Win work.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "20586d86a285"}
{"id": "lesson_66d2f9a8432a", "claim": "When git pull is blocked by local commits, use git pull --rebase origin main. If the local commit content is already upstream (e.g. a duplicate episodic memory entry), rebase drops it silently with 'dropping...patch contents already upstream' — this is correct, not data loss.", "conditions": ["already upstream", "divergent branches", "episodic memory", "git pull", "rebase"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-06-28T07:13:59.139511+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-06-28T15:13:59.185516", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "2026-06-28: PT had a dirty AGENT_LEARNINGS.jsonl committed locally. git pull failed with 'fatal: Need to specify how to reconcile divergent branches.' git pull --rebase resolved it; the commit was dropped because the upstream already had that content (pushed by an earlier session).", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "66d2f9a8432a"}
{"id": "lesson_601a930aa10d", "claim": "T5 tagging procedure: git tag v1.1.1 -m '<message>' then git push origin v1.1.1 in BOTH repos in the same session. No version bump needed if pyproject.toml/\\_version.py already reflect the target version. Tag only after ALL E2E gates pass (Mac E2E + Win E2E + Mac→Win cross-probe).", "conditions": ["E2E gate", "T5", "both repos", "git tag", "release tag", "v1.1.1"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-06-28T07:14:05.725890+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-06-28T15:14:05.769308", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "2026-06-28: Both repos were at version 1.1.1.0. No existing v1.1.x tags existed. T5 was unblocked once mac→Win curl probe succeeded. Tags pushed to GitHub without force-push since they were new.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "601a930aa10d"}
{"id": "lesson_18f5ca481f90", "claim": "When git commit output is piped through '| tail -N', a commit failure can look like success because the error message is truncated. Always check 'git status --short' after a commit that used tail-truncated output to confirm the file is no longer staged.", "conditions": ["LINT-006", "commit failure", "git commit", "pipe", "tail truncation"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-06-28T07:14:12.143512+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-06-28T15:14:12.189177", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "2026-06-28: 'git add ... && git commit ... 2>&1 | tail -3' showed 'Everything up-to-date' (the push message) while the file was still staged — the commit had failed due to LINT-006 but the error was cut off. Only discovered on second attempt with full output.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "18f5ca481f90"}
