{"id": "lesson_legacy_875b49541bed", "claim": "Explicit, dated, human-authorized exception to RED-first TDD for the remaining PR3 (coordination-bias-detector) findings in the endpoint-policy/tri-stack standardization effort, active from 2026-08-23. Scope is narrow and does not relax TDD as a general practice: only the still-open bias_detector.py findings (wrong event_type query target, missing word-boundary in substring confidence matching, incorrect variance metric for agreement_collapse, N+1 query pattern reading the full table then discarding via LIMIT in Python, and the dead agent_outputs parameter) may be fixed without RED-first tests. Fix only, commit in logical batches (one concern per commit, matching the discipline already used for PR1 and PR2 in this same stacked-PR effort), and push -- but proper RED-first regression tests for these specific fixes must be written and added by Thursday, 2026-08-27 (4 days from the exception's start date), before this work is considered complete. This does not apply to any other file, PR, or future work in this repo; the standing docs/TDD.md policy resumes immediately once these PR3 fixes are committed. The repo's own scripts/git/check_tdd_commit.sh gate is scoped only to web/src/*.ts(x) and never technically fires for Python files like bias_detector.py, so this exception isn't mechanically enforced either way here -- but its established `tdd-skip: <reason>` commit-message convention is followed in spirit for these commits, matching the documented policy in docs/TDD.md rather than inventing an ad-hoc format.", "conditions": [], "evidence_ids": [], "status": "legacy", "accepted_at": "2026-08-24T11:32:55.501419+00:00", "reviewer": "render_lessons_migration", "rationale": "Imported from pre-restructure LESSONS.md bullets below sentinel", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 5.0, "confidence": 0.7, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": null}
{"id": "lesson_49a2ac1933d9", "claim": "Remote telemetry exporters must reuse the canonical connection-time pinned transport and disable environment proxies; preflight URL validation alone does not close DNS rebinding or proxy boundary shifts.", "conditions": ["dns-rebinding", "observability", "otlp", "proxy", "ssrf"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-24T13:19:46.508723+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-24T13:19:46.720431+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "PT observability closure tests showed that OTLP accepted unsafe endpoints and its requests session inherited proxy routing until configure_otel_exporter injected SSRFPinnedHTTPAdapter with trust_env disabled.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "49a2ac1933d9"}
{"id": "lesson_399a47787f8b", "claim": "Before publishing a Git tree through an API, require the constructed remote tree SHA to equal the locally verified tree SHA; chunk large blobs instead of trusting truncated command output.", "conditions": ["ci", "observability", "review-remediation", "security", "ssrf", "git-tree-api", "tree-sha-verification", "large-blob-publication", "truncated-output"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-24T21:20:59.704398+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-24T21:20:59.767586+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "A prior observability commit appeared to delete a massive file set because an incomplete remote tree was constructed from truncated memory content. Exact tree-SHA equality prevented branch publication until the full blobs were recreated.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "399a47787f8b"}
{"id": "lesson_1044af2ba9f8", "claim": "Optional integrations need a stable module surface even when their dependency is absent, and CI must exercise that absence rather than relying on a developer environment with the dependency installed.", "conditions": ["ci", "observability", "review-remediation", "security", "ssrf", "optional-dependency", "dependency-absent-import", "stable-module-surface", "clean-interpreter"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-24T21:20:59.994208+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-24T21:21:00.066410+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "OpenTelemetry was installed locally but absent in GitHub CI, so monkeypatched tests failed before exercising the exporter. Defining explicit optional placeholders and adding an import-absence regression made the test seam portable.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "1044af2ba9f8"}
{"id": "lesson_fa960a6235d2", "claim": "When disabling Requests environment trust to prevent proxy-based SSRF boundary shifts, preserve explicit CA configuration first: REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE takes precedence and CURL_CA_BUNDLE is the fallback.", "conditions": ["ci", "observability", "review-remediation", "security", "ssrf", "requests-trust-env", "requests-ca-bundle", "curl-ca-bundle", "proxy-inheritance", "ssrf-boundary"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-24T21:21:00.289011+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-24T21:21:00.363499+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "trust_env=False correctly disables inherited proxies but also stops Requests from discovering private CA bundles. Assigning session.verify before disabling trust keeps mTLS-adjacent private-CA deployments available without reopening proxy inheritance.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "fa960a6235d2"}
{"id": "lesson_5ea2a39c2b12", "claim": "A security race regression is only evidence when it perturbs the production branch actually used on that platform; descriptor-relative POSIX tests should inject after the directory descriptor opens and assert fail-closed behavior if the pathname is replaced.", "conditions": ["ci", "observability", "review-remediation", "security", "ssrf", "posix-symlink-race", "descriptor-relative-write", "production-control-flow", "fail-closed-test"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-24T21:21:00.568546+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-24T21:21:00.627813+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "The first Periscope race test patched tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile, which the POSIX implementation never uses. Patching _open_local_directory exposed the real result: an unlink-and-symlink replacement cannot redirect the write and instead fails closed.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "5ea2a39c2b12"}
{"id": "lesson_cb77c6bf20a4", "claim": "Cross-repository security contracts must state only enforceable platform guarantees, link every remediation commit, and update the companion ADR whenever review changes the implementation's actual failure mode.", "conditions": ["ci", "observability", "review-remediation", "security", "ssrf", "cross-repository-contract", "platform-guarantee", "companion-adr", "remediation-evidence"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-24T21:21:00.842950+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-24T21:21:00.935788+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "The first ADR overclaimed HTTPS scope and portable race safety. It now requires HTTPS for every configured endpoint, scopes late-symlink guarantees to POSIX descriptor operations, labels the portable guard best-effort, and records the final PT evidence.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "cb77c6bf20a4"}
{"id": "lesson_4a711949b3ed", "claim": "GraphPlugin Protocol's full provenance, traced to primary sources in orama-system (not PT), recorded here for cross-repo traceability since the investigation started from a PT-adjacent MiniGraph reconciliation effort. Chain, each link verified via git metadata directly, not inferred: docs/v2/05-feasibility-review.md (orama-system, created 2026-05-01 by cyre) first names the exact method signatures on_node_start/on_node_end, recommending a formal GraphPlugin protocol specifically to prevent plugins \"monkey-patching the ainvoke loop\" and to give the SqliteCheckpointer and HITL Interrupts plugins \"documented hooks\" instead of reaching into MiniGraph internals directly -- an explicitly named leaky-abstraction concern, not an arbitrary design choice. docs/v2/08-technical-architecture-review.md (orama-system, created 2026-05-02, same commit 4ae30fe6/32d32743/19e948d5 that added GraphPlugin to 01-kernel-spec.md) implements 05's recommendation near-verbatim in its own section F1, and its title literally contains \"(Gemini-Analyzer)\" -- an explicit label, not informal attribution -- naming the AI collaborator used for this synthesis pass, committed under cyre's own git identity (same pattern as Kimi/Codex/Grok elsewhere in this ecosystem: a human using an AI collaborator, then committing the output themselves). F1's own stated rationale is standardizing an internal SPI for exactly two named consumers (Checkpointer, Interrupts), explicitly framed as differentiating from LangGraph/CrewAI's own patterns (\"succeeds where other frameworks fail\"), not mimicking either -- an earlier hypothesis that GraphPlugin was modeled on LangChain's BaseCallbackHandler is not supported by the primary source and should not be treated as established.", "conditions": ["added", "ae30fe6", "ainvoke", "arbitrary", "attribution", "basecallbackhandler", "chain", "checkpointer", "choice", "codex", "collaborator", "commit", "committed", "committing", "concer