{"id": "lesson_1687311690d7", "claim": "Supersedes the \"rule cannot be located\" claim in lesson_9bcca08eefa4, lesson_4a711949b3ed, lesson_e0ff7f2d6717, and lesson_1079e8c74f20 -- all four repeated a search failure across multiple turns (checked orama-system's CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, and .coderabbit.yaml only) and never checked .cursor/rules/, a standard Cursor IDE rule-file location that should have been checked from the first search, not the fourth attempt. The rule genuinely exists: orama-system/.cursor/rules/common-coding-style.mdc, frontmatter \"alwaysApply: true\" (global, not path-scoped), verbatim: \"ALWAYS create new objects, NEVER mutate existing ones\" with the contrasting pseudocode example modify(original, field, value) -> changes original in-place [WRONG] vs update(original, field, value) -> returns new copy with change [CORRECT]. Stated rationale: \"Immutable data prevents hidden side effects, makes debugging easier, and enables safe concurrency.\" This directly, unambiguously governs MiniGraph.add_node/add_edge, which lesson_1079e8c74f20 reverted to mutating self and returning self on the (mistaken) premise that no such rule existed to conflict with real LangGraph compatibility evidence. The LangGraph-compatibility evidence itself remains correct and unaffected (8/8 real-world sources use bare, non-reassigned add_node calls) -- what changed is that the rule conflict is now confirmed real, not hypothetical, and must be resolved against verified, real constraints on both sides rather than one side being falsely absent.", "conditions": ["absent", "across", "add_edge", "add_node", "against", "agents", "all", "always", "alwaysapply", "attempt", "bare", "both", "calls", "cannot", "change", "changed", "changes", "checked", "claim", "claude", "coderabbit", "common-coding-style", "compatibility", "concurrency", "confirmed", "conflict", "constraints", "contrasting", "copy", "correct", "create", "cursor", "data", "debugging", "directly", "easier", "effects", "enables", "evidence", "example", "existed", "existing", "exists", "failure", "falsely", "field", "first", "four", "fourth", "frontmatter", "genuinely", "global", "governs", "hidden", "hypothetical", "ide", "immutable", "in-place", "itself", "langgraph", "langgraph-compatibility", "lesson_1079e8c74f20", "lesson_4a711949b3ed", "lesson_9bcca08eefa4", "lesson_e0ff7f2d6717", "located", "location", "makes", "mdc", "minigraph", "mistaken", "modify", "multiple", "mutate", "mutating", "never", "new", "non-reassigned", "now", "objects", "one", "ones", "only", "orama-system", "original", "path-scoped", "premise", "prevents", "pseudocode", "rather", "rationale", "real", "real-world", "remains", "repeated", "resolved", "returning", "returns", "reverted", "rule", "rule-file", "rules", "safe", "search", "self", "side", "sides", "sources", "standard", "stated", "such", "supersedes", "than", "true", "turns", "unaffected", "unambiguously", "update", "use", "value", "verbatim", "verified", "which", "wrong", "yaml"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-27T20:57:49.671529+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-27T20:57:53.635441+00:00", "reviewer": "host-agent", "rationale": "Direct operator correction locating the exact rule file after four prior failed search attempts -- the rule is real and must supersede the false-absence claim across all four prior lessons that repeated it.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "1687311690d7"}
{"id": "lesson_230c6d2c5a7e", "claim": "GitHub access discovery, the most consequential finding of this week: the oramasys org's GitHub REST API (api.github.com/*) returns 403 on EVERY endpoint -- including fully unauthenticated, zero-token requests -- for repos that are genuinely public (confirmed: github.com/oramasys, github.com/oramasys/perpetua-core both return 200 and are readable). This is a real org-level API-access policy, not a token-scope limitation, and it blocked over a week of direct verification of oramasys/perpetua-core PR #1 despite the repo being open-source. The actual workaround, found only this week: plain web_fetch against ordinary github.com web pages (org profile, /pulls list, individual /pull/N conversation pages) is NOT blocked by this policy at all -- it returns full page content including PR descriptions, commit lists, and inline CodeRabbit review comments. This was a genuine gap in approach across many prior sessions/turns, not a true access wall: the API was tried exclusively and repeatedly declared \"still blocked\" without ever testing whether a different tool (plain HTTP page fetch vs. REST API) could see around the same org policy. Confirmed directly via this method: oramasys/perpetua-core PR #1 (\"refactor(graph): final MiniGraph reconciliation\", diazMelgarejo, opened Aug 26 2026) is real, open, mergeable, currently at head commit 876a4581f1016735ec3bf1c1475cd11cc76a5bab (12 commits), with baseline main@8c063f4 recording 62 passing tests before this PR's changes. As of two independent checks this week (both returning identical head SHA, confirming no drift), two CodeRabbit review findings remain genuinely open and unfixed on that real PR: (1) PerpetuaState.merge()'s more precise delta-aliasing bug -- model_copy(deep=True) deep-copies the EXISTING model but applies update=delta afterward without deep-copying delta's own values, so a caller-held mutable reference passed in delta still aliases across state generations; orama-system's own docs (01-kernel-spec.md) were corrected to the complete fix (model_copy(update=copy.deepcopy(delta), deep=True), verified against all three isolation properties directly) but this correction has not yet been ported back into the real perpetua-core code as of this check; (2) .github/workflows/test.yml's actions/checkout@v4 step does not set persist-credentials: false, flagged CWE-522 by zizmor SAST -- pull-request-controlled test code can access the persisted contents token, unfixed as of this check.", "conditions": ["a4581f1016735ec3bf1c1475cd11cc76a5bab", "access", "across", "actions", "actual", "afterward", "against", "aliases", "all", "api", "api-access", "applies", "approach", "around", "aug", "back", "baseline", "before", "blocked", "both", "bug", "c063f4", "caller-held", "changes", "check", "checkout", "checks", "code", "coderabbit", "com", "comments", "commit", "commits", "complete", "confirmed", "confirming", "consequential", "content", "contents", "conversation", "copy", "corrected", "correction", "currently", "cwe-522", "declared", "deep", "deep-copies", "deep-copying", "deepcopy", "delta", "delta-aliasing", "descriptions", "despite", "diazmelgarejo", "different", "direct", "directly", "discovery", "docs", "drift", "endpoint", "ever", "every", "exclusively", "existing", "false", "fetch", "final", "finding", "findings", "fix", "flagged", "found", "full", "fully", "gap", "generations", "genuine", "genuinely", "github", "graph", "head", "http", "identical", "including", "independent", "individual", "inline", "into", "isolation", "kernel-spec", "limitation", "list", "lists", "main", "many", "merge", "mergeable", "method", "minigraph", "model", "model_copy", "more", "most", "mutable", "only", "open", "open-source", "opened", "orama-system", "oramasys", "ordinary", "org", "org-level", "over", "own", "page", "pages", "passed", "passing", "perpetua-core", "perpetuastate", "persist-credentials", "persisted", "plain", "policy", "ported", "precise", "prior", "pro