{"id": "lesson_15d8d3b390c8", "claim": "Before continuing work on a shared feature branch across sessions, always git fetch+pull (ff-only) first, even mid-task -- do not assume the remote state matches what you last pushed.", "conditions": ["before editing", "concurrent session", "git pull", "multi-agent collision", "shared branch"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T04:36:43.555174+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-30T04:36:43.834187+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "On diazMelgarejo/Claude-Desktop-LLM PR #1, a concurrent session (same identity, same day) pushed 3 more CodeRabbit-driven security fixes (IPv6-bracket loopback bypass, DNS-resolved-to-loopback trust bypass, provider-validation gap) to the same branch after my last push. Continuing to edit from stale local state would have silently dropped or conflicted with real security fixes. Caught only because I re-fetched before making further changes.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "15d8d3b390c8"}
{"id": "lesson_f9911f5b8e57", "claim": "When adding a new validation/deny check to an existing multi-branch security gate, insert it AFTER the pre-existing allow/deny decision, not before -- ordering changes which error code fires for already-covered cases and can silently break passing tests.", "conditions": ["diff verification", "endpoint policy", "run full test suite", "security check ordering", "validateAndPin"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T04:36:44.558275+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-30T04:36:44.703959+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Added an HTTPS-required-for-non-loopback check ahead of the existing allowRemoteLlm/host-allowlist check in endpoint-policy.ts's validateAndPin(). This flipped the thrown error code (scheme_disallowed instead of non_loopback_denied) for the plain deny-by-default case, breaking 3 previously-passing tests. Only caught because the full test suite was re-run immediately after applying the diff -- a diff/plan document should never be trusted as correct without a full build+test pass against the live code.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "f9911f5b8e57"}
{"id": "lesson_22093a47c729", "claim": "A stale GITHUB_TOKEN environment variable silently overrides a valid gh/git keyring credential over HTTPS, producing '401 Bad credentials' or 'Invalid username or token' even when 'gh auth status' shows a correctly logged-in account. Fix: unset GITHUB_TOKEN before git push / gh api calls, and re-unset every time since shell env does not persist across tool-call boundaries in this harness.", "conditions": ["GITHUB_TOKEN", "bad credentials", "gh auth", "git push failed", "keyring"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T04:36:45.072855+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-30T04:36:45.178015+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Hit this exact failure pushing to diazMelgarejo/Claude-Desktop-LLM: 'gh auth status' showed a valid keyring login for the right account, but git push still failed until GITHUB_TOKEN was unset and gh auth switch was re-run against the keyring account.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "22093a47c729"}
{"id": "lesson_a8314abe1daa", "claim": "Before hardcoding a new default model name in a provider config, verify it is actually pulled/loaded in the live runtime (e.g. curl the provider's own /api/tags or /v1/models) rather than assuming availability from a naming convention or another config file's reference to it.", "conditions": ["default model", "nvfp4", "ollama", "provider config", "verify before default"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T04:36:45.633220+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-30T04:36:45.740859+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Confirmed qwen3.5:9b-nvfp4 was genuinely present in the local Ollama instance via /api/tags before making it Claude-Desktop-LLM's OLLAMA_MODEL default, since nvfp4 quantization has real hardware prerequisites and a wrong assumption would have broken out-of-box UX for anyone using the new default.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "a8314abe1daa"}
{"id": "lesson_ebe2c3995415", "claim": "Promoting a personal-account repo (diazMelgarejo/<repo>) to 'canonical under oramasys' is done by fast-forward-pushing the oramasys GitHub fork's own main branch directly to the desired commit -- not by merging a PR from the personal repo into oramasys, and not by GitHub's repo-transfer feature. The personal repo and any open PRs against it are left untouched unless explicitly told to close/merge them.", "conditions": ["canonical repo", "fast-forward main", "oramasys fork", "personal repo left alone", "repo promotion"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T04:36:45.994061+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-30T04:36:46.096092+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Confirmed on Claude-Desktop-LLM: oramasys/Claude-Desktop-LLM already existed as a GitHub fork of diazMelgarejo/Claude-Desktop-LLM, and its main had already been fast-forward-pushed directly to the working branch tip by a prior session -- bypassing a PR merge into the stale personal-repo main entirely. Confirmed via AskUserQuestion this was the intended pattern (oramasys fork = new canonical, personal repo left alone) rather than a full repo-transfer or a merge-then-mirror.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "ebe2c3995415"}
{"id": "lesson_ed9964a08154", "claim": "When a user's instructions contain internally contradictory values (e.g. two different version-number formats for the same bump, or an ambiguous repo-location instruction), investigate the live/factual state first (check if referenced repos/branches actually exist, what state they're in) and THEN present AskUserQuestion with options grounded in those facts -- never guess a resolution or silently pick the 'most likely' reading.", "conditions": ["AFRP", "AskUserQuestion", "ambiguous instructions", "investigate before asking", "never guess"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T04:36:46.359569+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-30T04:36:46.460385+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "User's request said 'Bump version to 1.5.0.0' (invalid 4-part semver) in one line and 'release v1.5' in another, both a downgrade from the actual current 2.0.0, plus an ambiguous 'close out using the new oramasys repos' with no repo URL given. Checking GitHub first (oramasys/Claude-Desktop-LLM already existed as a fork, its main already fast-forwarded) turned a vague question into a sharp, well-grounded 4-question AskUserQuestion set the user could answer precisely and quickly.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "ed9964a08154"}
{"id": "lesson_d74d82b3b257", "claim": "A custom undici connect.lookup callback must handle BOTH dns.lookup callback forms -- (err, address, family) AND, when options.all is true, (err, [{address, family}, ...]) -- because Node 22's net.Socket.connect requests Happy-Eyeballs-style lookups (options.all===true) for any HOSTNAME target. IP-literal targets never invoke connect.lookup at all, so tests using only IP literals will pass while every real hostname-based request throws 'Invalid IP address: undefined' in production.", "conditions": ["DNS pinning", "connect.lookup", "happy eyeballs", "hostname vs IP literal test gap", "options.all", "undici"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T05:39:42.023844+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-30T05:39:42.189454+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Found on Claude-Desktop-LLM's endpoint-policy.ts pinnedDispatcher: the connect-time IP-pinning mechanism (built to close a DNS-rebinding TOCTOU gap) only implemented the bare (address, family) callback form. This broke every real request through the documented DEFAULT config (OLLAMA_URL=http://localhost:11434, a hostname) end-to-end against live Ollama, while 64/64 unit tests stayed green because every existing ephemeral-server test used a 127.0.0.1 IP literal, which Node's net module never routes through connect.lookup in the first place. Only surfaced when manually driving the actual compiled server over real stdio against a live local model -- confirms that policy/security code doing custom DNS/connection interception needs at least one hostname-target integration test, not just IP-literal unit tests.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "d74d82b3b257"}
{"id": "lesson_fba170814ea7", "claim": "Ollama and LM Studio CAN run concurrently on this Mac for light-to-moderate single-request workloads -- the documented Mac inference-mode table (Ollama=active/warm primary, LM Studio=passive/probe-only) is an operational convention/division-of-labor choice, not a hard technical impossibility.", "conditions": ["concurrent", "lm studio", "mac inference mode", "ollama", "resource contention", "same machine"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T05:44:37.079631+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-30T05:44:37.376899+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Directly tested 2026-08-30: with Ollama already holding qwen3.5:9b-mlx loaded in memory, launched LM Studio and loaded qwen3.5-9b-mlx there too. Both /v1/completions (LM Studio) and /api/generate (Ollama) responded correctly to real requests while the other was also active. Claude-Desktop-LLM's check_llm_status tool (queries both providers concurrently in one call) reported both connected with correct default models, no errors, no hang. Free memory was tight (~865MB free pages) but nothing failed for this single-request-at-a-time test. Does not rule out contention under sustained/heavy concurrent load from both engines at once -- only disproves the strict 'cannot run both, period' framing for light usage.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "fba170814ea7"}
{"id": "lesson_d0beabdc9b21", "claim": "Retraction: lesson_fba170814ea7's 'Ollama and LM Studio can run concurrently' claim was vacuous and should not be trusted as evidence. check_llm_status only calls each provider's lightweight healthCheck() (a GET to /api/tags or /v1/models), which proves the HTTP server is reachable -- it does NOT prove a model is actually resident in GPU/unified memory at that instant. Ollama unloads idle models within minutes (confirmed via 'ollama ps' showing a model, then empty output shortly after); the two providers were almost certainly never actually memory-resident at the same moment during that test. The user explicitly stopped a follow-up test that would have force-loaded and concurrently hammered both engines, citing hardware/thermal risk -- do not attempt to settle this by deliberately loading concurrent heavy inference load on both engines; if the question matters, it needs a deliberate, cautious, user-directed test, not an agent-initiated stress test.", "conditions": ["concurrent", "healthCheck is not memory residency", "lm studio", "no stress testing hardware", "ollama", "overclaim correction", "retraction"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T05:46:09.049780+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-30T05:46:09.180500+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Corrected same-session: user pointed out the flaw immediately, pasted 'ollama ps' output proving unload-on-idle, and explicitly refused a proposed concurrent-load verification test on hardware/safety grounds. Recording the retraction so this false confidence doesn't propagate.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": "lesson_fba170814ea7", "source_candidate": "d0beabdc9b21"}
{"id": "lesson_f60e9a3a7ade", "claim": "CLOSED, NEVER-REOPEN rule: NEVER run Ollama and LM Studio under real concurrent heavy inference load on this Mac (M2 Pro), and never design or run a test that would. This is not an open research question -- the user has direct first-hand experience of a prior agent doing exactly this and causing the laptop to overheat and force-shutdown, with no replacement available. Confirmed independently: M2 Pro units are documented to reach ~108C under sustained heavy load (8K video export case study), and Apple's MacBook Pro fan curves are tuned for quiet operation over aggressive cooling -- the chip runs very hot before throttling engages, which is exactly the profile that produces an emergency/force shutdown rather than a graceful slowdown. Two LLM engines holding large models in shared unified memory and sustaining heavy GPU load concurrently is precisely this failure mode. Superficial single-request 'both servers reachable' tests (e.g. calling each provider's lightweight healthCheck) are NOT evidence either way and must never be used to justify going further -- see the retraction in lesson_d0beabdc9b21.", "conditions": ["M2 Pro", "closed question", "concurrent", "force shutdown", "hardware safety", "lm studio", "never test", "ollama", "overheat", "thermal"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-30T05:51:39.282634+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-30T05:51:39.379103+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "User corrected an agent (this session, 2026-08-30) who initially treated this as 'genuinely open' after a weak healthCheck-only test. User stated plainly: an agent tried this before, the laptop overheated and force-shut-down, there is no replacement, and repeating it would ruin the hardware with zero benefit. Verified the PT .agent/memory, orama-system docs/LESSONS.md, and Claude's own memory contain NO prior record of that incident -- it existed only as the user's direct testimony, not as written history, which is exactly why it must be captured permanently now.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "f60e9a3a7ade"}
