{"id": "lesson_eb056e4ab737", "claim": "When fixing automated-review findings (CodeRabbit, etc.) across a multi-language port of the same logic (e.g. a Python core + TypeScript core implementing one contract), always check whether the SAME bug class exists in the sibling language/test file too, even if the review only flagged one side -- automated reviewers commonly miss the parallel copy.", "conditions": ["code review findings", "cross-language parity", "multi-language port", "sibling bug", "test fixture bug"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-01T04:04:30.368951+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-01T04:04:30.470788+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "On orama-system PR #338, CodeRabbit flagged a test-fixture bug in conformance.test.ts (makeEnv() silently enabling extra methods with no registered executor, producing unexpected attempt-log entries). The Python suite's test_conformance.py had the IDENTICAL bug in its equivalent test, never flagged by the review, confirmed red via an actual pytest run before fixing. Caught only because I habitually re-ran the full suite after applying review fixes rather than trusting the diff was complete.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "eb056e4ab737"}
{"id": "lesson_6bd737c6a512", "claim": "In a cross-language contract where a JSON/wire format represents 'value unknown' as null, a TypeScript guard written as `!== undefined` does NOT exclude null (null !== undefined is true in JS) -- it will fall through and get silently coerced (e.g. to 0 in a > comparison), producing a wrong decision. Use `!= null` (loose equality) to exclude both null and undefined in one check, or type the field explicitly as `T | null` and check that.", "conditions": ["JSON null convention", "cross-language contract", "loose equality", "null vs undefined", "typescript"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-01T04:04:30.792783+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-01T04:04:30.893706+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Real production bug found on orama-system PR #338: contentInsertionFramework.ts's automationJustified() checked estimated_run_seconds !== undefined, but the JSON policy's own convention for 'unknown estimate' is null. A null run-estimate slipped past the undefined-only guard and coerced to 0 in the subsequent > comparison, closing an automation gate based on a false 'run is instant' assumption instead of treating the value as unknown.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "6bd737c6a512"}
{"id": "lesson_96854673cb96", "claim": "When a function has both a required-precondition check (e.g. 'signature must be non-empty') and an early-return branch for a different condition (e.g. 'decision is blocked, return early'), validate the precondition BEFORE the early-return branch, not after -- otherwise the early-return path silently skips the precondition entirely, and that's usually the path most likely to reach production unnoticed.", "conditions": ["blocked path", "precondition before early return", "silent bypass", "validation order"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-01T04:04:31.167189+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-01T04:04:31.267695+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Real bug in both the Python and TypeScript CIDF cores (PR #338): execute_with_fallback/executeWithFallback checked 'if decision.blocked: return blocked-result' BEFORE checking 'if not signature: raise'. This meant an empty signature was silently accepted on every blocked decision (no eligible tool found) -- exactly the path most likely to occur in real usage without anyone noticing the missing signature, since blocked results don't execute anything anyway that would surface the problem.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "96854673cb96"}
{"id": "lesson_e73221f5a7b4", "claim": "An async function's 'I performed side-effect X, so set flag claimed=True to remember to clean it up on cancellation' pattern is NOT cancellation-safe if the flag is set on the line AFTER an awaited call that can itself be cancelled mid-flight after its underlying effect already happened. The cancellation-safe window is the gap between 'effect happened' (inside the awaited call, invisible to the caller) and 'caller's own flag reflects it' (the next line) -- a CancelledError landing in that exact gap causes the caller's cleanup handler to see claimed=False and skip releasing a resource that was actually already acquired.", "conditions": ["TOCTOU", "asyncio cancellation", "cancellation-safe", "claim acquisition race", "idempotency store", "python async"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-01T04:04:31.550117+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-01T04:04:31.649797+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Confirmed real, Major-severity finding on oramasys/oramasys PR #1's GatewayLifecycle.run(): 'existing = await self._store.claim(key)' can have the store's implementation internally reserve the key before the await resumes; if cancelled in that window, the local 'claimed' bool never becomes True, so the except-CancelledError handler's 'if claimed: abort()' guard skips releasing the reservation -- the key leaks permanently and every later identical request blocks forever. The PR explicitly listed 'cancellation-safe claim release' as an already-solved safety property; it wasn't, for this exact acquisition window. General fix pattern: make the store's own claim() atomic with respect to cancellation (e.g. use asyncio.shield around only the reservation, or have claim() itself return a token whose absence unambiguously means nothing was reserved).", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "e73221f5a7b4"}
{"id": "lesson_5951daa6d4f8", "claim": "To post to the PT/orama multi-agent coordination board (GossipBus), use orchestrator.gossip_bus.GossipBus().emit(event_type, payload) from an async context -- never a raw sqlite3 INSERT into the gossip table. emit() runs the payload through classify_and_redact() (privacy/governance filtering), handles event_uuid dedup via INSERT OR IGNORE, and schedules embedding for genuinely-new events. event_type is a Literal enum (dispatch/route/result/error/heartbeat/fleet_topology_*/split_brain_*) -- free-form agent status notes use event_type='heartbeat' with payload {'kind': 'agent_note', 'agent_id': ..., 'message': ...}, matching the one prior real-world usage found in the board's history. DB path: resolve via GossipBus()'s default (git-common-dir-relative .state/perpetua_core.db), not a hardcoded path.", "conditions": ["coordination board", "emit event", "gossipbus", "multi-agent", "post status update"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-01T04:04:31.916236+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-01T04:04:32.021605+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Needed to post a status update to keep other agents in the loop about Claude-Desktop-LLM's canonical-repo change and two open PRs with unresolved findings. Found the correct API by reading orchestrator/gossip_bus.py directly rather than assuming a raw INSERT was safe -- a raw insert would have skipped redaction and the FTS/embedding pipeline other tooling depends on.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "5951daa6d4f8"}
{"id": "lesson_7b260b9c1ce5", "claim": "Before the first push in a multi-remote worktree, run 'git remote -v' and confirm which remote name maps to the intended target repo -- never assume 'origin' is correct when a worktree was set up with multiple named remotes (e.g. 'origin' for a frozen v1 repo, 'oramasys' for the real v2 code repo).", "conditions": ["gate4", "git-remote-verification", "multi-repo-worktree", "v2-regime-boundary"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-07T14:29:48.758154+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-07T14:29:48.858480+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Self-caught near-miss 2026-09-07: pushed a oramasys/oramasys code fix (192.88.99.0/24 dialer prohibition) to 'origin' by reflexive 'git push -u origin <branch>', which resolved to diazMelgarejo/orama-system (the frozen v1 docs-only repo) instead of the 'oramasys' remote pointing at oramasys/oramasys. Caught before opening a PR; deleted the wrong branch and re-pushed to the correct remote. No v1 contamination occurred, but the regime boundary (v1 repos receive zero agent-merged code) was one PR-open away from being violated.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "7b260b9c1ce5"}
{"id": "lesson_66b917484d5e", "claim": "When a user says 'do not merge anything', treat that as banning every git-merge operation (including a local branch-into-branch merge to consolidate two PRs), not only 'do not merge to main via GitHub'. If an instruction to consolidate PRs doesn't explicitly say how (cherry-pick individual commits vs. git merge vs. keep separate), ask rather than defaulting to the mechanism that's convenient for the agent -- a git merge combines two PRs' history irreversibly-in-spirit (closing one, as I did) even though the actual command is local and reversible in git terms.", "conditions": ["git-merge-scope", "orama-system", "pr-consolidation", "user-instruction-ambiguity"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-09T11:42:52.649916+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-09T11:42:52.745079+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "2026-09-09: user said 'wait, continue ALL on paused work, just do not merge anything, but reuse the same relevant branch?' -- intended meaning was keep PT/orama-system PR #350 and #352 fully separate, only keep adding my own fixes to my own branch (#352). I instead ran git merge origin/pr350 into my branch and closed #350, collapsing two PRs into one. User explicitly corrected this and said not to roll it back, but to remember the distinction for next time.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "66b917484d5e"}
{"id": "lesson_5efb8cefb8af", "claim": "Telos is the sole reusable v2 endpoint-security authority succeeding Tripwire; it owns endpoint identity, SSRF and DNS-rebinding defense, pinned transport, redirect, proxy and TLS destination safety, and endpoint-use authorization, while PT remains an independent v1 authority and v2 consumers must not reimplement secure connectors.", "conditions": ["dns-rebinding", "endpoint-security", "proxy", "redirects", "socket-pinning", "ssrf", "telos", "tls", "tripwire", "v1-v2"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-09T21:41:38.100378+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-09T21:41:38.207175+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Restores the owner-approved 2026-08-29 architecture after the semantic-only September scaffold drift. Evidence: orama-system PR 351 ADR 62; oramasys/telos PR 1 exact head 509d38299011d82f8df14e577169898bdebc344c with Python 3.11/3.12 CI success; Claude-Desktop-LLM PR 1 exact head 6a602e8786708dbe62a112360ddde4ba63b8c3ad with CI success; phylax PR 1 exact head 9c5ad79e95c0400a0e24ef7c4f5d6fd90a9b27c5 with CI success; PT v1 endpoint-policy, ssrf_fetch_policy and ssrf_pinned_adapter remain clean-room evidence only.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "5efb8cefb8af"}
{"id": "lesson_legacy_6cc23d837789", "claim": "Two real security bugs found and fixed in diazMelgarejo/Claude-Desktop-LLM PR #1 (branch 2026-08-29-canonical-typescript-modernization, commits cb082af and 9a8c063), both worth auditing for in any v2/oramasys-org port of the same patterns, since this repo is TypeScript and forking into oramasys/* will involve re-deriving or re-porting this logic again. (1) src/tools/handlers.ts's selectProvider() treated an omitted provider and an invalid supplied provider identically -- both silently fell through to ctx.config.activeProvider, so { provider: \"invalid\" } on any provider-selecting tool (e.g. local_llm_query) ran against activeProvider instead of erroring. Reachable despite the MCP tool JSON schemas declaring provider: { enum: [\"ollama\",\"lmstudio\"] } for every such tool -- schema hints describe intent to well-behaved clients but the server had no independent enforcement between receiving a raw tool-call request and dispatching to handleToolCall. Fixed with one validation gate at the shared call site before selectProvider, covering every provider-selecting tool through the same switch statement. General lesson for any MCP tool server: never treat a declared JSON-schema enum as the actual enforcement boundary -- validate server-side independently, at the one shared dispatch point if the codebase has one, not per-tool. (2) src/policy/endpoint-policy.ts's validateAndPin() computed addressIsLoopback = isLoopbackAddress(resolvedIp) || isLoopbackHostname(hostname) and skipped ALL of ALLOW_REMOTE_LLM/ALLOWED_LLM_HOSTS/HTTPS enforcement whenever this was true -- meaning a hostname that merely resolves to 127.0.0.1 via DNS got FULL loopback trust, identical to a hostname the caller directly wrote as \"localhost\" or \"127.0.0.1\". Concretely exploitable: an attacker who controls DNS for their own domain (they always do, for domains they own) can point it at 127.0.0.1 and get complete bypass of every remote-endpoint protection with zero allowlisting and zero HTTPS requirement, because the connection is pinned to the resolved IP (closing the classic DNS-rebinding TOCTOU gap) but the original attacker-chosen hostname is preserved for the Host header/TLS SNI -- this is a real security-boundary bypass, not cosmetic. There is no legitimate case where a genuinely-remote, allowlisted, HTTPS endpoint should ever resolve to loopback. Fixed with isDirectLoopbackSpecification(): true only for a recognized loopback name or a literal loopback IP the caller wrote directly, never based on a DNS lookup result; a hostname that is not itself loopback but resolves there is now rejected unconditionally, before any allow/allowlist/HTTPS check can permit it. The comparative architecture finding, more consequential than either individual fix: this TypeScript file's own doc comment says it \"mirrors Perpetua-Tools' src/utils/ssrf_pinned_adapter.py,\" but checking that actual Python file (and its SSOT deny predicate in src/utils/ssrf_fetch_policy.py) shows a fundamentally different, more robust design -- _is_denied_ip checks addr.is_loopback (Python's own ipaddress module property) on the ACTUAL RESOLVED address as one of several uniformly-denied ranges (loopback/private/link-local/CGNAT/multicast/reserved), with an explicit, separate allowlist mechanism layered on top for legitimate local-LLM use, and has NO hostname-string-based shortcut anywhere. The TypeScript port introduced a hostname-string classification path (isLoopbackHostname checking for \"localhost\"/\"*.localhost\") that grants automatic trust BEFORE uniformly checking the resolved address's actual range, and that architectural divergence from the original pattern is exactly where this vulnerability was introduced during the port. Bulk-remediation implication for v2: when forking or re-deriving this pattern into oramasys/* repos, audit for this specific divergence class -- \"does the port classify by resolved-address-range uniformly (safe), or does it grant early trust based on the hostname string before resolution (unsafe)\" -- in any language, not just re-copy the TypeScript file's current logic and assume it matches the canonical Python pattern.", "conditions": [], "evidence_ids": [], "status": "legacy", "accepted_at": "2026-09-10T16:36:21.874100+00:00", "reviewer": "render_lessons_migration", "rationale": "Imported from pre-restructure LESSONS.md bullets below sentinel", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 5.0, "confidence": 0.7, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": null}
