{"id": "lesson_legacy_7dc92839bb52", "claim": "Shell hygiene — correct wait patterns: (1) run_in_background: true tasks — receive notification, then read output; no sleep needed. (2) File condition polling — until grep -q 'done|error|added|Updated|failed' file; do sleep 3; done. (3) File size polling — until [ -gt N ]; do sleep 2; done. (4) PID wait — until ! kill -0 PID; do sleep 2; done. (5) Port/service up — until curl -s http://host:port/health >/dev/null; do sleep 2; done.", "conditions": [], "evidence_ids": [], "status": "legacy", "accepted_at": "2026-09-10T16:36:21.874100+00:00", "reviewer": "render_lessons_migration", "rationale": "Imported from pre-restructure LESSONS.md bullets below sentinel", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 5.0, "confidence": 0.7, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": null}
{"id": "lesson_legacy_2bfd113c7b78", "claim": "Shell hygiene — zsh word splitting: zsh does NOT word-split unquoted (SH_WORD_SPLIT off). WRONG: for id in (fires once with all IDs joined); cmd (all files as one arg). CORRECT: printf '%s\\n' \"\" | while IFS= read -r id; do resolve \"\"; done; files=(a.py b.py); cmd \"\". Always quote expansions; these forms work identically in bash and zsh.", "conditions": [], "evidence_ids": [], "status": "legacy", "accepted_at": "2026-09-10T16:36:21.874100+00:00", "reviewer": "render_lessons_migration", "rationale": "Imported from pre-restructure LESSONS.md bullets below sentinel", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 5.0, "confidence": 0.7, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": null}
{"id": "lesson_c2cfa44e28df", "claim": "GitHub write-access asymmetry between REST and GraphQL for review-thread management, discovered on a token with contents:write only (no pulls:write): the GraphQL mutation resolveReviewThread(input:{threadId}) genuinely succeeds and persists (confirmed via a follow-up query showing isResolved:true) even though every REST write endpoint tested this whole session -- posting issue comments, posting PR comments, closing/patching PRs, and the specific pulls/{n}/comments/{id}/replies endpoint -- returns 403 \"Resource not accessible by personal access token.\" The asymmetry is narrow, not a general GraphQL bypass: addPullRequestReviewThreadReply (posting a reply comment via GraphQL) returns an explicit FORBIDDEN error, identical in effect to the REST 403s. So resolving a thread is possible without pulls:write; posting any comment, by any path tried, is not. Practical implication: when a review thread needs marking resolved after a fix lands, try the GraphQL resolveReviewThread mutation directly rather than assuming REST-blocked means completely blocked -- but don't expect to also leave an explanatory reply; that still needs pulls:write and the person's own action.", "conditions": ["access", "accessible", "action", "addpullrequestreviewthreadreply", "after", "also", "any", "assuming", "asymmetry", "between", "blocked", "bypass", "closing", "comment", "comments", "completely", "confirmed", "contents", "directly", "discovered", "don", "effect", "endpoint", "error", "even", "every", "expect", "explanatory", "explicit", "fix", "follow-up", "forbidden", "general", "genuinely", "github", "graphql", "identical", "implication", "input", "isresolved", "issue", "lands", "leave", "management", "marking", "means", "mutation", "narrow", "needs", "only", "own", "patching", "path", "persists", "person", "personal", "possible", "posting", "practical", "prs", "pulls", "query", "rather", "replies", "reply", "resolved", "resolvereviewthread", "resolving", "resource", "rest", "rest-blocked", "returns", "review", "review-thread", "session", "showing", "specific", "still", "succeeds", "tested", "than", "though", "thread", "threadid", "token", "tried", "true", "try", "via", "whole", "without", "write", "write-access"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-02T00:48:55.128416+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-02T00:49:01.018430+00:00", "reviewer": "host-agent", "rationale": "New, actionable GitHub access-capability discovery narrowing what future sessions should try before assuming a write action is fully blocked.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "c2cfa44e28df"}
{"id": "lesson_bb17e2c4e576", "claim": "Methodology for \"add durable protection against a class of bug\" requests: a config file alone is not durable if nothing actually runs it. Concretely on orama-system's CIDF directory: the initial ask was framed as \"add a tsconfig.json\" to close a specific gap (a future TS consumer forgetting a default-value guard, silently producing wrong behavior since undefined comparisons evaluate to false with no exception). Adding the tsconfig and verifying `tsc --strict` catches the class of bug would have been a plausible-looking complete answer, but tracing the actual CI workflow (.github/workflows/ci.yml) revealed the deeper, real gap: CI never executed any TypeScript in this directory at all (no tsc, no jest, no npx -- only a one-line Python import smoke test), and pytest's testpaths never covered bin/orama-system/cidf/tests/, so a real, passing 37-test conformance suite had never once run in CI. The durable fix required three coupled things, not one: (1) the config file itself, (2) fixing whatever real issues the newly-strict check immediately surfaces on first run (config alone against untested code will surface genuine findings, not just noise -- confirmed directly: noUncheckedIndexedAccess found a real unprovable-non-empty-array case; mypy --strict found real untyped-dict and invalid-callable-annotation issues), (3) wiring the check into the actual CI job graph including its blocking dependency chain (a new job that isn't in any downstream job's `needs` list runs but never blocks anything, which defeats the entire purpose). General pattern: when asked to \"add a safety config\" for X, always trace whether X's classification/config is genuinely enforced end-to-end (present -> runs in CI -> blocks merge) before treating the fix as complete, not just whether the config file exists and passes when run manually.", "conditions": ["actual", "actually", "add", "adding", "against", "all", "alone", "always", "answer", "any", "anything", "ask", "asked", "before", "behavior", "bin", "blocking", "blocks", "bug", "case", "catches", "chain", "check", "cidf", "class", "classification", "close", "code", "comparisons", "complete", "concretely", "config", "confirmed", "conformance", "consumer", "coupled", "covered", "deeper", "default-value", "defeats", "dependency", "directly", "directory", "downstream", "durable", "end-to-end", "enforced", "entire", "evaluate", "exception", "executed", "exists", "false", "file", "findings", "first", "fix", "fixing", "forgetting", "found", "framed", "future", "gap", "general", "genuine", "genuinely", "github", "graph", "guard", "immediately", "import", "including", "initial", "into", "invalid-callable-annotation", "isn", "issues", "itself", "jest", "job", "json", "just", "list", "manually", "merge", "methodology", "mypy", "needs", "never", "new", "newly-strict", "noise", "nothing", "nouncheckedindexedaccess", "npx", "once", "one", "one-line", "only", "orama-system", "passes", "passing", "pattern", "plausible-looking", "present", "producing", "protection", "purpose", "pytest", "python", "real", "requests", "required", "revealed", "run", "runs", "safety", "silently", "since", "smoke", "specific", "strict", "suite", "surface", "surfaces", "test", "testpaths", "tests", "things", "three", "trace", "tracing", "treating", "tsc", "tsconfig", "typescript", "undefined", "unprovable-non-empty-array", "untested", "untyped-dict", "verifying", "whatever", "whether", "which", "wiring", "workflow", "workflows", "wrong", "yml"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-02T00:48:55.181321+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-02T00:49:01.198589+00:00", "reviewer": "host-agent", "rationale": "The single most generalizable insight from this whole exchange, applicable to any future 'add durable protection' request regardless of language or repo.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candid