{"id": "lesson_1ba604860660", "claim": "Two real, reusable mypy technical gotchas found while adding strict type-checking to orama-system's bin/orama-system/cidf/ (relevant to any repo with a similarly hyphenated directory or dual/inconsistent import styles): (1) a directory name containing a hyphen (orama-system) is not a valid Python package identifier, so mypy fails immediately with \"contains __init__.py but is not a valid Python package name\" unless run with --namespace-packages and --explicit-package-bases (or the pyproject.toml [tool.mypy] equivalents namespace_packages=true, explicit_package_bases=true) -- this is not a bug in the code, it's a structural fact about the directory name that must be worked around in tooling config. (2) When the same importable module is reachable from two different MYPYPATH roots under two different dotted names (e.g. a file importable as both core.content_insertion_framework via one root and cidf.core.content_insertion_framework via another, because two files in the same codebase genuinely use inconsistent import styles for the same package), mypy fails with \"Source file found twice under different module names\" if both roots are given in a single invocation. Fix: run mypy separately per file-group, using only the MYPYPATH root that group's own imports actually rely on, and add --follow-imports=silent to a later invocation that transitively reaches files an earlier invocation already fully checked -- this avoids re-triggering the same ambiguity while still getting full checking on each group's own signatures. Do not try to force one unified invocation to handle both import styles at once; that's fighting the codebase's own real inconsistency rather than working around it cleanly.", "conditions": ["about", "actually", "add", "adding", "already", "ambiguity", "another", "any", "around", "avoids", "because", "bin", "both", "bug", "checked", "checking", "cidf", "cleanly", "code", "codebase", "config", "containing", "contains", "content_insertion_framework", "core", "different", "directory", "dotted", "dual", "each", "earlier", "equivalents", "explicit-package-bases", "explicit_package_bases", "fact", "fails", "fighting", "file", "file-group", "files", "fix", "follow-imports", "force", "found", "full", "fully", "genuinely", "getting", "given", "gotchas", "group", "handle", "hyphen", "hyphenated", "identifier", "immediately", "import", "importable", "imports", "inconsistency", "inconsistent", "init__", "invocation", "later", "module", "mypy", "mypypath", "name", "names", "namespace-packages", "namespace_packages", "once", "one", "only", "orama-system", "own", "package", "per", "pyproject", "python", "rather", "re-triggering", "reachable", "reaches", "real", "relevant", "rely", "repo", "reusable", "root", "roots", "run", "same", "separately", "signatures", "silent", "similarly", "single", "source", "still", "strict", "structural", "styles", "technical", "than", "toml", "tool", "tooling", "transitively", "true", "try", "twice", "two", "type-checking", "under", "unified", "unless", "use", "using", "valid", "via", "while", "worked", "working"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-02T00:48:55.235986+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-02T00:49:01.387984+00:00", "reviewer": "host-agent", "rationale": "Specific, reusable mypy technical knowledge that saves rediscovering two real gotchas from scratch in this or a similarly-structured repo.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "1ba604860660"}
{"id": "lesson_6a4ce9f61cc8", "claim": "Supersedes lesson_1ba604860660, narrowing its two mypy claims to only the exact tested context, per direct review feedback that the original overclaimed generality. Both findings were verified specifically against: repo diazMelgarejo/orama-system, working directory the repo root, Python 3.12.3, mypy 2.3.1 (note: repo's own pyproject.toml pins mypy~=1.13, and the real CI environment installed 1.20.2 -- a different version than what was locally verified; this record does not claim the finding reproduces under 1.20.2, only under the locally-tested 2.3.1). Finding 1: running `python -m mypy bin/orama-system/cidf/core/content_insertion_framework.py bin/orama-system/cidf/linter/policy_linter.py bin/agents/executor/execution_tools.py --strict --namespace-packages --explicit-package-bases` from the repo root fails with \"orama-system contains __init__.py but is not a valid Python package name\" -- caused specifically by bin/orama-system/__init__.py existing alongside the hyphenated directory name bin/orama-system, under this exact mypy version and flag combination; not verified as a general mypy behavior across other versions or invocation styles. Finding 2: running mypy with MYPYPATH set to both \"bin/orama-system\" and \"bin/orama-system/cidf\" simultaneously in one invocation, when both bin/orama-system/cidf/core/content_insertion_framework.py (importable as core.content_insertion_framework via one root) and bin/orama-system/cidf/linter/policy_linter.py's own internal `from core.content_insertion_framework import ...` (reached transitively via the other root as cidf.core.content_insertion_framework) are both reachable, fails with \"Source file found twice under different module names\" -- specific to this repo's own inconsistent internal import styles (policy_linter.py uses a bare `core.X` import relying on its own sys.path.insert() hack, while execution_tools.py imports the same package via the full `cidf.core.X` path), not a general two-MYPYPATH-roots problem. The fix verified for both (separate invocations, MYPYPATH scoped per file group, --follow-imports=silent on the transitively-reached group) is specific to this exact layout and was not tested against any other repo structure or mypy version.", "conditions": ["across", "against", "agents", "alongside", "any", "bare", "behavior", "bin", "both", "caused", "cidf", "claim", "claims", "combination", "contains", "content_insertion_framework", "context", "core", "diazmelgarejo", "different", "direct", "directory", "environment", "exact", "execution_tools", "executor", "existing", "explicit-package-bases", "fails", "feedback", "file", "finding", "findings", "fix", "flag", "follow-imports", "found", "full", "general", "generality", "group", "hack", "hyphenated", "import", "importable", "imports", "inconsistent", "init__", "insert", "installed", "internal", "invocation", "invocations", "layout", "lesson_1ba604860660", "linter", "locally", "locally-tested", "module", "mypy", "mypypath", "name", "names", "namespace-packages", "narrowing", "note", "one", "only", "orama-system", "original", "other", "overclaimed", "own", "package", "path", "per", "pins", "policy_linter", "problem", "pyproject", "python", "reachable", "reached", "real", "record", "relying", "repo", "reproduces", "review", "root", "running", "same", "scoped", "separate", "set", "silent", "simultaneously", "source", "specific", "specifically", "strict", "structure", "styles", "supersedes", "sys", "tested", "than", "toml", "transitively", "transitively-reached", "twice", "two", "two-mypypath-roots", "under", "uses", "valid", "verified", "version", "versions", "via", "while", "working"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-02T04:15:13.904517+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-02T04:15:20.355571+00:00", "reviewer": "host-agent", "rationale": "Direct review feedback correctly identified the original lesson generalized findings only tested against one specific repo layout, interpreter, and mypy version. Superseding with exact tested context rather than editing in place.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": "lesson_1ba604860660", "source_candidate": "6a4ce9f61cc8"}
{"id": "lesson_034b1745dcdb", "claim": "For periscope (diazMelgarejo/periscope): the repo has two closely-related but genuinely distinct branches, main and merged, and confusing them is a real, demonstrated failure mode, not theoretical. A fresh git clone defaults to main. Confirmed directly this session: main can be genuinely far behind merged (695 commits at time of check) while still being an ancestor (not diverged) -- meaning fixes built against a default clone can target already-resolved bugs without any merge-conflict signal to catch the mistake, since the branches never diverged, main just stopped moving forward. Concretely: 4 CI job failures were investigated (a month-old Benchmark Gate run plus a coverage/lint/integration set on main at a specific commit); several real-looking fixes were built and verified against that clone before the branch mismatch was caught via git merge-base --is-ancestor <sha> origin/merged. Re-verified against merged's actual current tip: all 4 were already resolved there (a file that no longer exists in that form; a Scan()-vs-column-count mismatch already fixed independently; golangci-lint already clean; the same DB bug feeding the integration failures). The real, current CI status for merged's own tip, checked directly via GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/commits/{sha}/check-runs, showed only one job (build-and-push, success) -- the coverage/lint/integration jobs apparently only run on pull requests, not direct pushes to merged, so \"merged's CI is green\" is a narrower claim than \"every job type recently ran and passed.\" Required discipline before touching any code in response to a reported CI failure in this repo: (1) confirm which branch the failing job's commit actually belongs to -- do not assume a default clone or a branch name in a URL is the right target; (2) check how old the job run is; (3) check the real current tip's own CI status directly via the API before diagnosing, since a local clone's checkout may not match. A smaller, related technical gotcha found along the way: a naive Python .split(\",\") on a raw SQL column-list string produces a false positive column-count mismatch when the list contains a COALESCE(a, b) AS c expression (comma inside parens) -- any script comparing SQL column lists to Go Scan() destinations in this codebase needs a parenthesis-aware splitter, not a naive one.", "conditions": ["actual", "actually", "against", "all", "along", "already", "already-resolved", "ancestor", "any", "api", "apparently", "assume", "before", "behind", "belongs", "benchmark", "branch", "branches", "bug", "bugs", "build-and-push", "built", "catch", "caught", "check", "check-runs", "checked", "checkout", "claim", "clean", "clone", "closely-related", "coalesce", "code", "codebase", "column", "column-count", "column-list", "comma", "commit", "commits", "comparing", "concretely", "confirm", "confirmed", "confusing", "contains", "coverage", "current", "default", "defaults", "demonstrated", "destinations", "diagnosing", "diazmelgarejo", "direct", "directly", "discipline", "distinct", "diverged", "every", "exists", "expression", "failing", "failure", "failures", "false", "far", "feeding", "file", "fixed", "fixes", "form", "forward", "found", "fresh", "gate", "genuinely", "get", "git", "golangci-lint", "gotcha", "green", "independently", "inside", "integration", "investigated", "is-ancestor", "job", "jobs", "just", "lint", "list", "lists", "local", "longer", "main", "match", "meaning", "merge-base", "merge-conflict", "merged", "mismatch", "mistake", "mode", "month-old", "moving", "naive", "name", "narrower", "needs", "never", "old", "one", "only", "origin", "own", "owner", "parens", "parenthesis-aware", "passed", "periscope", "plus", "positive", "produces", "pull", "pushes", "python", "ran", "raw", "re-verified", "real", "real-looking", "recently", "related", "repo", "reported", "repos", "requests", "required", "resolved", "response", "right", "run", "same", "scan", "script", "session", "set",