{"id": "lesson_fad3af10b7cd", "claim": "Supersedes lesson_757476abb44e: GitHub authorization evidence must bind token type to the exact method and endpoint; a linked user profile or repository admin/push metadata does not describe a GitHub App installation token's endpoint permissions, a PR-metadata 403 does not prove merge denial, and every 403 diagnosis must capture X-Accepted-GitHub-Permissions or state that the exact missing permission remains unproven.", "conditions": ["endpoint-authorization", "evidence-boundary", "fine-grained-pat", "github-app", "installation-token", "supersedes", "x-accepted-github-permissions"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-02T22:10:53.736243+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-02T22:10:53.797300+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Review of the 2026-09-02 Periscope incident found that the prior lesson conflated the linked repository-owner profile with the connector's installation identity and grouped PR metadata and merge permissions without a merge-endpoint request. Observed evidence supports Contents and collaborator endpoint denial for the App token, and PR PATCH denial for the fine-grained PAT; response permission headers were not captured.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": "lesson_757476abb44e", "source_candidate": "fad3af10b7cd"}
{"id": "lesson_5491895b9770", "claim": "A non-empty diff and a matching parent/child tree delta prove that SOME content changed at a destination -- they do not prove the INTENDED artifact reached it. This is a real, distinct gap from (and one step beyond) the earlier empty-commit lesson (lesson_fee9305e811e, extending lesson_9fac191b11e1): even when a commit's tree genuinely differs from its parent (ruling out the empty-commit failure mode already documented), the diff could still be the WRONG content -- a stale cached version, a different file entirely, a partial write -- and \"the tree changed\" alone would not catch it. Confirmed via a real review finding on orama-system PR #339 (pullrequestreview-5095683687): the existing mandatory git-publication-verification sequence in bin/orama-system/afrp/failure-modes.md stopped at `git show --stat --oneline HEAD` (confirms a non-empty diff exists) and the equivalent AFRP SKILL.md proxy-table row stopped at \"compare parent/child trees\" -- both insufficient by the review's own precise framing. Fixed by adding a mandatory final verification step to both: `git show HEAD:<path> | grep -q \"<expected-marker>\"`, confirming the SPECIFIC intended content (a distinctive string, section heading, or function signature -- not a generic pattern that would also match unrelated content) is actually present in the committed blob, not just that some diff occurred. Verified the exact command pattern both directions (matches a real marker; correctly reports absence for a fabricated one) before writing it into the docs, rather than assuming the pattern would work. General, reusable rule for any content-publication workflow, not specific to git: after every write operation, verify the actual destination contains the specific expected content marker, not merely that the write \"succeeded\" or that some observable signal (HTTP 200, new SHA, non-empty diff, changed byte count) occurred -- those are all transport evidence, not content evidence. Caught and fixed a real inaccuracy in my own first draft of the doc explanation before committing: initially described a specific commit as having \"a different parent but wrong content,\" when its actual documented defect was a zero tree delta from its real parent -- a materially different claim; corrected by rereading my own addition critically before pushing, not because it was flagged by someone else.", "conditions": ["absence", "actual", "actually", "adding", "addition", "afrp", "after", "all", "alone", "already", "also", "any", "artifact", "assuming", "because", "before", "beyond", "bin", "blob", "both", "byte", "cached", "catch", "caught", "changed", "child", "claim", "command", "commit", "committed", "committing", "compare", "confirmed", "confirming", "confirms", "contains", "content", "content-publication", "corrected", "correctly", "count", "critically", "defect", "delta", "described", "destination", "diff", "different", "differs", "directions", "distinct", "distinctive", "doc", "docs", "documented", "draft", "earlier", "else", "empty-commit", "entirely", "equivalent", "even", "every", "evidence", "exact", "existing", "exists", "expected", "expected-marker", "explanation", "fabricated", "failure", "failure-modes", "file", "final", "finding", "first", "fixed", "flagged", "framing", "function", "gap", "general", "generic", "genuinely", "git", "git-publication-verification", "grep", "having", "head", "heading", "http", "inaccuracy", "initially", "insufficient", "intended", "into", "just", "lesson", "lesson_757476abb44e", "lesson_fad3af10b7cd", "mandatory", "marker", "match", "matches", "matching", "materially", "merely", "mode", "new", "non-empty", "observable", "occurred", "one", "oneline", "operation", "orama-system", "out", "own", "parent", "partial", "path", "pattern", "precise", "present", "prove", "proxy-table", "pullrequestreview-5095683687", "pushing", "rather", "reached", "real", "reports", "rereading", "reusable", "review", "row", "rule", "ruling",