{"id": "lesson_cb52a6a3600d", "claim": "Preserves the non-authorization claims from lesson_757476abb44e, which lesson_fad3af10b7cd's narrower supersession would otherwise remove from structured recall entirely (recall._load_structured() excludes an ENTIRE superseded id, not just the specific claim a narrower successor actually corrects -- confirmed directly by reading .agent/tools/recall.py: \"if lid in retired: continue\" happens before any content-scope comparison). fad3af10b7cd only corrects the authorization-evidence-binding claim; it says nothing about the watcher deadlock, the hand-waving diagnostic failure, or PR-locality/non-merge discipline, so those three claims needed their own accepted record before the supersession could be relied on safely.\n\n(1) Windows fsnotify watcher-cleanup deadlock and the correct diagnostic method: a Windows CI Go test (TestEnsureBackgroundServeReplacesIncompatibleDaemonAfterStartupWait, periscope) was initially diagnosed as flaky from a proxy signal alone -- passed on one commit, failed on a later one, zero relevant code diff between them -- and concluded \"flaky, just re-run it\" without ever opening the actual assertion or tracing the watcher-cleanup code path. The real root cause, found only by someone who actually traced the code: Windows cleanup called the native fsnotify Remove on a watch already invalidated by a prior remove/rename event, hanging watcher shutdown -- a genuine, deterministic deadlock reachable under a specific event ordering, not a timing coin-flip. Fixed by retaining ownership/budget cleanup while skipping the redundant native Remove call, with a regression test proving no second native removal occurs. General lesson: absence of a diff in the diagnosing agent's own recent commits is not evidence a bug doesn't exist, and a passing/failing sample of one commit each is never sufficient evidence to distinguish \"flaky\" from \"deterministic but rarely exercised by CI's actual scheduling.\" Cross-referenced 