{"id": "lesson_b85d462f63ae", "claim": "Never label a CI failure flaky or recommend a rerun from proxy signals alone. First read the failing assertion and trace the exercised code path; a passing/failing sample with no local diff can still be a deterministic bug whose triggering event order is uncommon.", "conditions": ["ci-triage", "flaky", "handwaving", "proxy-conclusion", "root-cause"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-03T19:48:57.157394+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-03T19:48:57.238720+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Separated from lesson_757476abb44e so the anti-hand-waving method remains structured recall guidance after the authorization-only supersession.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "b85d462f63ae"}
{"id": "lesson_legacy_092997ae1643", "claim": "Preserves the non-authorization claims from lesson_757476abb44e, which lesson_fad3af10b7cd's narrower supersession would otherwise remove from structured recall entirely (recall._load_structured() excludes an ENTIRE superseded id, not just the specific claim a narrower successor actually corrects -- confirmed directly by reading .agent/tools/recall.py: \"if lid in retired: continue\" happens before any content-scope comparison). fad3af10b7cd only corrects the authorization-evidence-binding claim; it says nothing about the watcher deadlock, the hand-waving diagnostic failure, or PR-locality/non-merge discipline, so those three claims needed their own accepted record before the supersession could be relied on safely. (1) Windows fsnotify watcher-cleanup deadlock and the correct diagnostic method: a Windows CI Go test (TestEnsureBackgroundServeReplacesIncompatibleDaemonAfterStartupWait, periscope) was initially diagnosed as flaky from a proxy signal alone -- passed on one commit, failed on a later one, zero relevant code diff between them -- and concluded \"flaky, just re-run it\" without ever opening the actual assertion or tracing the watcher-cleanup code path. The real root cause, found only by someone who actually traced the code: Windows cleanup called the native fsnotify Remove on a watch already invalidated by a prior remove/rename event, hanging watcher shutdown -- a genuine, deterministic deadlock reachable under a specific event ordering, not a timing coin-flip. Fixed by retaining ownership/budget cleanup while skipping the redundant native Remove call, with a regression test proving no second native removal occurs. General lesson: absence of a diff in the diagnosing agent's own recent commits is not evidence a bug doesn't exist, and a passing/failing sample of one commit each is never sufficient evidence to distinguish \"flaky\" from \"deterministic but rarely exercised by CI's actual scheduling.\" Cross-referenced as a third example under orama-system's own AFRP Failure Mode 7 (Handwaving/Proxy Conclusion), bin/orama-system/afrp/failure-modes.md, commit f67c2f1b. (2) PR-locality and non-merge discipline (kept genuinely separate from authorization diagnosis, which lesson_fad3af10b7cd now covers on its own): a discovery/investigation PR (periscope #49) accumulating a real production fix does not thereby become authorized to merge -- discovery, publication, review, and merge authorization are four distinct decisions, and \"save/commit/push/publish/fix\" never implies \"merge.\"", "conditions": [], "evidence_ids": [], "status": "legacy", "accepted_at": "2026-09-03T19:48:57.086506+00:00", "reviewer": "render_lessons_migration", "rationale": "Imported from pre-restructure LESSONS.md bullets below sentinel", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 5.0, "confidence": 0.7, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": null}
