"accepted\": continue` runs BEFORE the supersedes field is even read. This means setting a `supersedes` field on a row with status=\"legacy\" (or \"provisional\", or anything else) has ZERO rendering/exclusion effect -- confirmed by testing the wrong direction first and catching it before committing, not by reading the source alone. If you need to retire a non-\"accepted\" row (e.g. an auto-migrated status=\"legacy\" duplicate from render_lessons.py's stray-bullet migration pass), the fix runs in the OPPOSITE direction from what's intuitive: create or reuse an ACCEPTED record whose own `supersedes` field points AT the row you want retired, not the reverse. (3) VERIFY BY EXECUTION, not by reading code and reasoning about what it should do: after patching lessons.jsonl directly (a case where a hand-edit was appropriate -- fixing a structured field on an existing, not-yet-externally-built-upon record, matching this repo's own established exception), the fix was confirmed by actually importing and calling recall._load_structured() directly in a Python one-liner and checking the real output list, not by re-reading the JSONL and asserting it \"should\" work. This caught nothing wrong here, but the discipline is the point: for any fix to retrieval/rendering logic, run the actual consuming function against the actual current file state before considering the fix complete. (4) CHECK REVIEW TIMING BEFORE ASSUMING A CONTRADICTION IS REAL: a review comment appeared to directly contradict a fix already verified and pushed minutes earlier. Rather than assume either the review or the prior verification was wrong, checked the review object's own `commit_id` field via the GitHub API against the actual current branch tip -- confirmed the review was posted against an older commit, one push behind. This resolved the apparent contradiction cleanly and is a reusable check for any \"this review says X is broken but I just verified X is fixed\" situation: compare timestamps/commit SHAs dir