{"id": "lesson_432f535474c5", "claim": "Never assume Python's ipaddress predicates (is_reserved, is_private, is_global) map cleanly onto security-policy intent -- verify each predicate directly on the interpreter in use before writing address-classification logic that gates network egress", "conditions": ["address-classification", "ipaddress", "python-stdlib", "security-policy", "verification"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-07T11:44:47.173706+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-07T11:44:47.259110+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "CPython 3.13's ipaddress.ip_address('::1').is_reserved is True, which silently rejected IPv6 loopback as a prohibited address in a dial-authorization gate -- a real functional bug that survived three review passes (author, CodeRabbit, an independent adversarial review) because everyone trusted the predicate name over checking its actual behavior.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "432f535474c5"}
