{"id": "lesson_5869d08b2179", "claim": "Locked D2–D5 operational decisions remain active independently of the revised D1 versioning lesson: keep runtime-agent configuration distinct from methodology-agent configuration; retain the integration feature scope and supported platform/install coverage; retain documented authentication precedence and its non-interactive fallback; and retain documentation for both command-line and supervisor HTTP entrypoints. Do not reopen these decisions without an explicit human override.", "conditions": ["D2 D3 D4 D5 locked decisions operational continuity human override"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-15T05:30:36.778753+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-15T05:30:36.901334+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "The revised D1 lesson supersedes an older compound decision. This standalone continuation preserves the unaffected D2–D5 guidance without relying on partial supersession semantics.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "5869d08b2179"}
{"id": "lesson_6f2a3c1d9e47", "claim": "Final MiniGraph mutation/observer reconciliation superseding the remaining overstatements in lesson_e0ff7f2d6717 and extending lesson_1079e8c74f20. The previously hard-to-locate blanket rule was found in orama-system/.cursor/rules/common-coding-style.mdc as an alwaysApply instruction: 'ALWAYS create new objects, NEVER mutate existing ones'. That rule is now explicitly superseded on orama PR #333 by a boundary-aware policy: immutable/copy-on-write semantics for values, versioned specifications, and published snapshots; intentional local mutation is allowed for documented builder/workspace/buffer/cache APIs when it cannot leak across snapshot boundaries. MiniGraph therefore remains a mutable construction builder whose add_node/add_edge mutate self and return self; compile() is the detached immutable runtime boundary. Persistent structural sharing is retained as a good idea but relocated to the future versioned GraphSpec layer, where with_node/with_edge-style persistent updates fit identity, diffing, optimization, and promotion semantics. PerpetuaState.merge() is now implemented in oramasys/perpetua-core PR #1 with model_copy(update=delta, deep=True), closing the verified nested-container aliasing bug while preserving the rule that nodes/observers treat received state as immutable input. Observer R2.4 is corrected: every registered plugin must be DELIVERED every GraphObservation in deterministic order, but semantic plugins may ACT on different subsets (e.g. Checkpointer persists node.end while Tracer records the full structural stream). Regression proof is split into identical spy-plugin delivery, heterogeneous semantic filtering, and plugin-enabled-vs-no-plugin final-state parity. The one-scheduler architecture remains _run() -> GraphObservation -> aobserve()/GraphEvent->asteps(), with GraphPlugin multicast draining aobserve() exactly once. GraphSpec validation remains fail-closed in orama-system. Process-only merge-conflict mechanics from lesson_e0ff7f2d6717 remain valid but are not MiniGraph architecture.", "conditions": ["extends-lesson_1079e8c74f20"], "evidence_ids": [], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-28T04:30:00+08:00", "reviewer": "host-agent", "rationale": "A blanket no-mutation rule was finally located and shown to be the source of the builder conflict. The final design keeps immutability where it is semantically load-bearing and preserves established builder semantics where mutation is the contract. Observer delivery and plugin-specific action are separated explicitly, eliminating the prior R2.4 overconstraint.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 9.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": "lesson_e0ff7f2d6717", "source_candidate": "6f2a3c1d9e47"}
{"id": "lesson_7b5d3e91a4c2", "claim": "MiniGraph/orama resumability is retained as an explicit R4 target. The canonical classification is: LangGraph checkpoint/thread identity ADOPT; atomic successful-boundary checkpoints ADOPT/ADAPT; durable resumability ADOPT R4 target; the phrase 'perfect resumption' REJECT WORDING ONLY; durable deterministic resume ADOPT TARGET. A wording correction must not be interpreted as feature deletion. The intended guarantee is deterministic continuation from an explicit compatible saved session/graph boundary with checkpoint lineage, graph/version/schema identity, replay policy, and effect identity/idempotency/dedupe. This is deliberately not total reversibility or time travel: graph-state restoration cannot erase external side effects that already occurred, which require idempotency, dedupe, compensation, or explicit human/policy handling. In reconciliation documents, distinguish feature disposition from wording disposition and treat deferred as not implemented in the current PR, not rejected.", "conditions": ["resumability", "resume", "checkpoint", "session-state", "langgraph", "perfect-resumption", "durable-resume", "r4", "replay", "idempotency", "dedupe"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-08-28T00:00:00+08:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-08-28T00:00:00+08:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Backfilled 2026-09-12 (PT PR#385 review 5184262838): this record's accepted_at/reviewer/rationale were null despite status=accepted, bypassing graduate.py's required-rationale gate -- context_budget's _top_lessons() admits any status=accepted row regardless. No original review record survives from the 2026-08-28 architecture-week session that produced this lesson; backfilled using its own evidence_ids timestamp as the best available acceptance signal, not a rediscovered original review.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 9.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candid