{"id": "lesson_02c40b9ea724", "claim": "agent_coordination heartbeat cleanup releases only legacy agent_claim rows; the distributed-queue exclusion gate (task_claims) is cleaned separately by heartbeat_monitor's cleanup_stale_queue_claims -- verified directly against liveness_status()'s thresholds (LIVENESS_STALLED_SEC=1800s, unchanged since this feature was introduced): an 80h-silent agent is classified DEAD (30min threshold), not IDLE, so cleanup_stale_queue_claims() DOES remove its task_claims row and emit a requeue event with status QUEUED automatically -- manual fail-as-claimant recovery is NOT required for this case. (This reverses an earlier, unverified version of this same lesson that wrongly claimed 80h silence could be misclassified IDLE and skipped; that claim was checked against source and found false -- corrected here, see PT PR #385 review 5184262838.)", "conditions": ["heartbeat", "liveness", "stale-claim", "task_claims"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-11T21:01:56.868799+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-11T21:01:56.955323+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "gate4-f07-port-allowlist stayed claimed after cleanup ran; heartbeat_monitor classified its 80h-silent claimant IDLE, so auto-release skipped it; manual fail path closed it cleanly. CORRECTION (2026-09-12, PT PR#385 review 5184262838): the original IDLE-misclassification claim above was verified against heartbeat_monitor.py directly and found factually wrong -- LIVENESS_STALLED_SEC=1800s has never changed, so 80h silence has always classified DEAD, and cleanup_stale_queue_claims() does auto-release+requeue DEAD claimants. The claim field was corrected in place rather than superseded, since this is a data-integrity fix to a still-current record, not a new finding.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "02c40b9ea724"}
{"id": "lesson_9c27e7dc69c7", "claim": "MiniGraph converts a graph node's Interrupt into an interrupted PerpetuaState (status=interrupted, interrupt_node/interrupt_prompt/interrupt_payload metadata), it does NOT propagate to the ainvoke caller — assert on the returned state, and never record an interrupt as a failure outcome in ledgers because it is structural HITL control flow", "conditions": ["graph-testing", "hitl", "interrupt", "minigraph"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-11T21:02:07.690140+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-11T21:02:07.778233+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "First outbound-ledger test wrongly expected pytest.raises(Interrupt); existing transport-contract tests and a rerun against real MiniGraph semantics showed the interrupted-state shape.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "9c27e7dc69c7"}
{"id": "lesson_8320ab46363d", "claim": "asyncio.wait_for raises a builtin TimeoutError whose str() is empty — at any containment boundary translate it into an explicit RuntimeError carrying the bound (e.g. provider invocation timed out after Ns) so error deltas and append-only ledgers record a real message", "conditions": ["asyncio", "error-messages", "ledger", "timeout", "wait_for"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-11T21:02:07.892385+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-11T21:02:07.978316+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "Timeout test caught 'provider invocation failed: ' with no message; translating at the seam gave both the error delta and the ledger record a readable, bounded message.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "8320ab46363d"}
{"id": "lesson_5800cf2c280e", "claim": "When a board packet names a source SHA, never reuse a worktree that other live agents share — they can commit under you mid-review (HEAD moved and the working tree changed between two read commands in one session); create a fresh worktree from the exact source_ref SHA even if a checked-out worktree already exists", "conditions": ["concurrency", "multi-agent", "source-ref", "worktree"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-11T21:02:30.356528+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-11T21:02:30.442457+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "During the Agate review another agent committed two slices and the bindings work appeared/disappeared between tool calls; the fresh-worktree rule from the board-job source-line protocol would have avoided the moving-ground hazard.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "5800cf2c280e"}
{"id": "lesson_608576b75a5e", "claim": "RED-first discipline: write the failing test and capture its failure output BEFORE implementing; the captured RED evidence (collection error or assertion output) belongs in the board completion notes and PR body, not just the final green count", "conditions": ["evidence", "red-first", "tdd", "testing"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-11T21:02:30.553462+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-11T21:02:30.637960+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "The outbound-ledger lane captured the collection-error RED state before implementing; reviewers and the board could verify the tests actually failed first — cheap, high-trust evidence.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "608576b75a5e"}
{"id": "lesson_75f7eaa356e9", "claim": "Before rotating any credential in response to another agent's 'push blocked by stale/exposed credential' report, verify the actual failure first: run 'gh auth status' and a 'git push --dry-run' in the current session. A report from a different agent session is frequently a shadowed/stale GITHUB_TOKEN env var in THAT session's shell, not a genuinely compromised or expired credential -- this repo's own convention of unsetting GITHUB_TOKEN before every gh/git call already avoids the failure mode being reported.", "conditions": ["cross-session-handoff", "git-credentials", "verification-before-action"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-11T21:58:07.407882+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-11T21:58:07.502889+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "2026-09-12: a handoff report claimed a stale Git HTTPS credential blocked push to oramasys/agate PR #2, recommending rotation. Verified directly instead of rotating -- gh auth status showed a valid active keyring token, and a dry-run push succeeded cleanly. Pushed the actual commit with zero auth issues. Rotating a working credential on an unverified report would have been unnecessary churn and a real security-adjacent action taken without cause.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "75f7eaa356e9"}
{"id": "lesson_c660861f37c1", "claim": "When asked to fix a specific CodeRabbit review finding by URL/ID, fetch that exact review's comments and diff against the PR's CURRENT head before writing any code -- a relayed finding is frequently already resolved (the repo owner or another agent fixed it since the finding was captured), and CodeRabbit itself posts an 'Addressed in commit <sha>' confirmation directly in the comment thread when that happens. Check for that confirmation and verify the cited commit is actually reachable from current HEAD before assuming work remains.", "conditions": ["coderabbit-findings", "pr-review", "stale-instructions", "verification-before-action"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-11T21:58:14.181389+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-11T21:58:14.268956+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "2026-09-12: given 3 explicit CodeRabbit fix requests across oramasys/oramasys#6, oramasys/agate#2, oramasys/perpetua-core#4. All 3 were already fixed and CodeRabbit-confirmed at each PR's current HEAD -- verified via git log/API rather than blindly re-applying the pasted diffs, which would have been redundant no-op commits at best and a conflicting/stale-base edit at worst.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "c660861f37c1"}
{"id": "lesson_93c1b7416ff3", "claim": "For any consequential action against mutable external authority (especially PR creation, merge decisions, or branch recovery), treat cached memory, a prior lookup, and a branch name as non-authoritative: immediately re-read current state, exact base/head SHAs, source-repository identity, and remaining delta; then re-read after the write. Missing, null, or truncated search output proves nothing, and creating a PR never authorizes merge, direct-main writes, deletion, deploy, or later side effects.", "conditions": ["afrp", "branch-recovery", "github", "live-authority", "mutable-state", "post-write-verification", "pr-identity"], "evidence_ids": ["2026-09-12T05:19:34.617286+00:00"], "status": "accepted", "accepted_at": "2026-09-12T05:19:34.683572+00:00", "reviewer": "learn.py", "rationale": "2026-09-12 stale-GitHub-state incident: a merged Agate PR was mistakenly described as open/duplicate from stale context. The corrective AFRP PR #356 was merged to Orama System main as 39988ce; a fresh Core sweep also showed the apparent pending Telos/GraphSpec branch already merged. The same read-classify-diff-reread sequence prevents duplicate PRs and authority overreach.", "cluster_size": 1, "canonical_salience": 8.0, "confidence": 0.6, "support_count": 0, "contradiction_count": 0, "supersedes": null, "source_candidate": "93c1b7416ff3"}
